WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:47.054
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:47.054 --> 00:00:52.531
I grew up in Lyubertsy, a small town near Moscow.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.352
A small town, it borders Moscow.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.302
And the first few years,
I'd go to school in my town,

00:00:59.402 --> 00:01:03.165
and for the last two years, I'd
go to math school in Moscow.

00:01:04.298 --> 00:01:10.050
Actually, I wasn't really interested in math,
I was more into history and literature,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:16.291
but parents nudged me toward math
because they knew that for Jewish children,

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:23.543
it was nearly impossible to get into a university
pursuing a History of Literature degree.

00:01:24.035 --> 00:01:28.389
And the only way to higher education
was a technical degree.

00:01:28.957 --> 00:01:35.851
That's why I went to math school and then enrolled
in the Applied Mathematics program at an institute,

00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:41.877
got a technical degree, but the interest
in Humanities somehow took over.

00:01:42.354 --> 00:01:46.857
And first I got interested in the Philosophy
of Science, then History of Science,

00:01:48.145 --> 00:01:56.353
I wrote a dissertation on the history
of AI research in the United States.

00:01:56.775 --> 00:02:01.418
While I was living in Russia, I was
an expert in American science.

00:02:02.116 --> 00:02:08.162
But then I enrolled for a doctoral degree
at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology

00:02:08.162 --> 00:02:11.489
majoring in History and Social Studies of Science and Technology

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:20.819
and came here already having one degree from Russia
— a scientific degree of a candidate of philosophical sciences.

00:02:21.742 --> 00:02:26.978
And then here, I wrote another dissertation,
now as a historian of Russian science —

00:02:27.275 --> 00:02:30.268
a dissertation on the history of Soviet cybernetics.

00:02:30.579 --> 00:02:37.369
Ironically, the object of my study was always
on the other side of the Atlantic from me.

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:46.493
And after that, this dissertation was published as a book
From Newspeak to Cyberspeak: A History of Soviet Cybernetics.

00:02:46.987 --> 00:02:58.112
And then... it's typical for historians of science
to change their field of interest every few years.

00:02:58.204 --> 00:03:05.568
That's why when I finished my project on the history of Soviet
cybernetics, I took up the history of the Soviet space program.

00:03:06.335 --> 00:03:10.165
And I spent many years working on this topic.

00:03:10.537 --> 00:03:15.749
But the last few years, life has
lead to my return to mathematics

00:03:15.749 --> 00:03:22.615
and now I work in the math department at MIT.
And I live and work among mathematicians, so to speak.

00:03:22.775 --> 00:03:30.438
And my current research project is based
on oral interviews with mathematicians.

00:03:30.438 --> 00:03:36.264
I have already interviewed more than 90 mathematicians
on the topic Social History of Soviet Mathematics.

00:03:36.264 --> 00:03:49.978
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:49.978 --> 00:03:53.121
I must say that I was not
a particularly athletic child.

00:03:53.369 --> 00:03:58.258
I liked to read books, so I
enjoyed reading science fiction

00:03:58.347 --> 00:04:01.562
and thus joined in on the cosmic dreams.

00:04:01.794 --> 00:04:08.164
Although I collected space-themed stamps, so I guess
I already had some kind of attraction to space.

00:04:08.757 --> 00:04:21.598
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:21.598 --> 00:04:27.735
Interest in the space program initially stemmed from
my interest in cybernetics and computing machinery,

00:04:28.154 --> 00:04:33.794
because I approached this topic from
the side of using onboard computers

00:04:33.972 --> 00:04:37.667
in the US Apollo space program.

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:43.894
As you know, the Apollo lunar landing was possible
thanks to the use of an onboard computer —

00:04:44.124 --> 00:04:46.360
the first computer
on integrated circuits.

00:04:46.742 --> 00:04:52.576
And it was the history of the Appolo
onboard computer that I worked on.

00:04:53.298 --> 00:04:57.445
And interviewed veterans of
the American space program.

00:04:57.687 --> 00:05:01.297
And then I became interested: what was
happening at this time in the Soviet Union,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:09.108
where, as you know, they also had a lunar
program (though secret), were computers used there?

00:05:09.147 --> 00:05:12.273
I became interested,
and I started to dig into this topic,

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:17.187
work in archives, interview veterans
of the Soviet space program.

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:22.493
And gradually this topic began to go
beyond just using computers,

00:05:22.594 --> 00:05:29.488
because it turned out that the Soviet Union was 20 years
behind in the field of onboard computers for spacecraft.

00:05:30.154 --> 00:05:36.969
And in this sense, I became interested:
how did they fly without such advanced technology?

00:05:39.143 --> 00:05:44.634
And then I wondered how Soviet
spacecraft were generally designed,

00:05:44.683 --> 00:05:51.187
what share of the functions was transferred
to automation, to some machines,

00:05:51.412 --> 00:05:57.232
and what share of the functions remained with
the cosmonaut and was given to manual control.

00:05:57.569 --> 00:06:00.312
It turned out that this is not a
simple technical question.

00:06:00.397 --> 00:06:08.724
This is a question that was open for
debate between engineers and cosmonauts,

00:06:08.762 --> 00:06:11.237
leaders of the Soviet space program.

00:06:11.262 --> 00:06:15.224
That is, to some extent, it reflected
not so much technical efficiency but

00:06:15.524 --> 00:06:21.225
rather the political weight that these groups
that participated in the negotiations had.

00:06:22.051 --> 00:06:24.604
Then the whole thing was starting to unravel,

00:06:24.647 --> 00:06:32.416
and I wrote a book on the history of the
Soviet space program called Soviet Space Myths,

00:06:32.782 --> 00:06:42.486
where the issue of computers and automation
is only one particular side of this big topic,

00:06:42.744 --> 00:06:47.642
which covers the interaction of various
groups within the Soviet space program:

00:06:47.709 --> 00:06:53.132
how they defined Soviet space policy,
how they interacted with the wider public.

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:57.609
So sort of a sociocultural history
of the Soviet space program.

00:06:57.808 --> 00:07:11.016
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:11.016 --> 00:07:15.167
Well, we all know about Sputnik —
at least we know this word.

00:07:15.357 --> 00:07:24.834
Launched October 1957 in the Soviet Union, Sputnik was
the first artificial body launched around the Earth.

00:07:24.884 --> 00:07:32.729
And it was launched on a rocket, which was
a modified intercontinental ballistic missile.

00:07:33.004 --> 00:07:41.707
In fact, the space program was an offshoot
of the military nuclear strike program,

00:07:42.159 --> 00:07:47.999
where these intercontinental ballistic missiles
were created in order to carry a nuclear payload

00:07:48.446 --> 00:07:50.556
and fly from the Soviet Union to America.

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:54.866
Really, this was the main goal of this program.

00:07:54.979 --> 00:08:00.956
And, after Sergei Korolev, the chief
designer of these missiles,

00:08:01.459 --> 00:08:07.712
managed to successfully test his
rocket — the famous "seven", R-7,

00:08:08.306 --> 00:08:13.134
there were still a few copies
of the missiles made for tests,

00:08:13.809 --> 00:08:20.582
and he asked for permission from the political leadership
of the Soviet Union to use them for space flights.

00:08:21.384 --> 00:08:23.169
It was a roots initiative.

00:08:23.331 --> 00:08:30.248
And the launch of Sputnik
thus began the space race.

00:08:31.721 --> 00:08:43.147
The Americans were catching up, and the
Soviet engineers launched first each time,

00:08:45.131 --> 00:08:47.883
had what you would call cosmic triumphs.

00:08:47.979 --> 00:08:53.124
After the first Sputnik was the
launch of the first cosmonaut,

00:08:53.349 --> 00:08:57.827
then the launch of a cosmonaut
who orbited for an entire day,

00:08:58.104 --> 00:09:03.453
then there was the launch of two spacecraft,
which were simultaneously in orbit,

00:09:03.659 --> 00:09:07.230
then there was the launch of a
spacecraft with three cosmonauts,

00:09:07.791 --> 00:09:12.843
the launch of a spaceship with a woman
on board and, finally, a spacewalk.

00:09:12.899 --> 00:09:18.962
All these are the famous Soviet space triumphs, which
were achieved in a short period — from 1961 to 1965.

00:09:20.971 --> 00:09:26.365
And all the while, the Soviet Union was giving the
impression that it was in the lead in this race.

00:09:26.794 --> 00:09:29.153
And, indeed, these achievements were the first.

00:09:29.186 --> 00:09:37.574
But at the same time, a more ambitious goal
was being worked on in the United States —

00:09:37.784 --> 00:09:44.884
landing and delivery of astronauts to the
surface of the Moon, their return to Earth.

00:09:44.929 --> 00:09:52.258
It was also a political task — to rehabilitate for the
defeat with Sputnik and the first cosmonaut Gagarin.

00:09:52.746 --> 00:09:54.996
And a lot of money has been invested in it.

00:09:55.334 --> 00:10:04.184
And, while the Soviet leaders of the space program
paid more attention to these space triumphs,

00:10:04.234 --> 00:10:10.026
leaders of the American program worked on
improving technology, on the Gemini program

00:10:10.266 --> 00:10:17.584
and then on the Apollo program, which led to the success
of the lunar space program in the United States.

00:10:17.649 --> 00:10:22.928
But the Soviet Union, having learned about
this, began to develop its own lunar program.

00:10:23.784 --> 00:10:26.616
Prior to this, the Soviet Union
focused on a flight to Mars,

00:10:26.671 --> 00:10:31.155
but now, since there is a lunar race,
they have refocused on the Moon.

00:10:31.569 --> 00:10:39.859
But then there is a very interesting story — how the competition arose within the Soviet space program

00:10:39.884 --> 00:10:48.481
which led to splitting the efforts of the Soviet Union in this lunar program,

00:10:48.536 --> 00:10:54.744
when two parallel programs were funded:
one to fly around the Moon and return to Earth,

00:10:54.783 --> 00:11:03.519
and the other — for the flight and landing — the
cosmonaut's walk on the Moon and return to Earth.

00:11:03.893 --> 00:11:09.958
It seems that these are very close programs,
but they were separated in the Soviet Union.

00:11:09.983 --> 00:11:16.043
They were developing... two completely different
rockets were made for this, different spacecraft,

00:11:16.393 --> 00:11:18.968
different groups of cosmonauts
were preparing for this.

00:11:19.031 --> 00:11:23.298
So the Soviet Union directed
efforts in two directions at once,

00:11:23.323 --> 00:11:28.631
and in the end, there were not enough resources
to sufficiently develop the technology,

00:11:28.681 --> 00:11:30.373
and the Americans were the first to go to the Moon.

00:11:30.768 --> 00:11:35.048
It would seem that that's it, it's the
final victory of American astronautics.

00:11:35.123 --> 00:11:41.298
But the spirit of victory quickly
disappears, something new must be done,

00:11:41.728 --> 00:11:51.601
and both countries found themselves in a situation where they had
to somehow show something, some new achievements after the Moon.

00:11:52.083 --> 00:11:57.744
And at that moment, the Soviet Union refocused
on the creation of orbital stations,

00:11:58.316 --> 00:12:01.583
and said: although the Americans send
people to the Moon, no one needs it,

00:12:01.621 --> 00:12:05.251
but we will launch an orbital station, and
we will conduct scientific research there.

00:12:07.653 --> 00:12:11.961
And at the same time, the joint
Apollo-Soyuz project was launched,

00:12:12.033 --> 00:12:16.905
where in 1975 two ships, an American
and a Soviet one, docked in orbit.

00:12:17.133 --> 00:12:22.003
It was also a very interesting symbolic act, and a technologically complex one,

00:12:22.053 --> 00:12:27.793
when they had to work out the docking of
two completely different technical devices.

00:12:27.911 --> 00:12:33.378
And such a docking that would
be symbolically equivalent,

00:12:33.423 --> 00:12:39.548
so that there are no active and passive ships,
as docking of spaceships was done before.

00:12:39.586 --> 00:12:48.494
Therefore, it was necessary to invent a new docking device,
which maintained the technological equality of the parties.

00:12:48.786 --> 00:12:55.393
So the politics was woven into technological
design at precisely this micro level.

00:12:55.723 --> 00:12:58.790
After that, a new race began — with the shuttle.

00:12:59.103 --> 00:13:03.118
A shuttle was launched in America, and the
Soviet Union started to develop a shuttle too.

00:13:03.168 --> 00:13:06.884
Few people know about this, but the
Soviet Union built its own shuttle

00:13:07.203 --> 00:13:13.386
that carried out a test flight in a completely
automatic mode, without cosmonauts on board —

00:13:13.836 --> 00:13:24.546
it launched, went into orbit and then returned and
landed completely autonomously, without cosmonauts.

00:13:24.723 --> 00:13:29.003
It was actually a technological breakthrough,
which was completely forgotten after that

00:13:29.066 --> 00:13:35.211
because this program was closed.
And the Soviet Union returned to

00:13:35.231 --> 00:13:41.589
launching old and already tested Soyuz
spacecraft to orbital stations.

00:13:41.856 --> 00:13:46.085
And then — a new round of cooperation.

00:13:46.528 --> 00:13:51.844
We see here that periods of competition
and periods of cooperation are alternating.

00:13:52.186 --> 00:13:56.481
And now a new period of cooperation —
the International Space Station

00:13:56.506 --> 00:14:06.028
which is still functioning and to a large extent supports
the two large space programs in the two countries,

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:11.692
provides a political purpose for the
existence of this project.

00:14:13.933 --> 00:14:22.072
And here we see — Soyuz fly there, and commercial
ships are already starting to fly from America.

00:14:22.465 --> 00:14:31.448
So new breakthrough technologies are
being built into the old canvas,

00:14:31.473 --> 00:14:36.544
but those that will probably outgrow it, since
this is the development of commercial technologies,

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:43.415
perhaps it will surpass this narrow function of delivering
cosmonauts and astronauts to the International Space Station

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:46.564
and will find a wider field of applications.

00:14:46.564 --> 00:14:59.443
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:14:59.443 --> 00:15:02.640
I am most familiar with
the Apollo-Soyuz program,

00:15:03.315 --> 00:15:06.893
because it was actually
the first cooperation program.

00:15:09.428 --> 00:15:19.808
And it was very difficult for two programs — to open up
and start talking with specialists from the other side,

00:15:19.833 --> 00:15:25.067
because they were used to treating their work as
something super secret that needs to be concealed.

00:15:25.393 --> 00:15:31.996
And now they had to find a common language,
somehow make themselves cooperate.

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:37.043
And it turned out that it's
actually not such a difficult thing,

00:15:37.073 --> 00:15:42.284
that specialists from both sides understand
each other very well, easily find a common language

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:44.528
and they are very interested
in working with each other.

00:15:46.293 --> 00:15:54.665
And teams of specialists from the States started coming to the Soviet Union,
and from the Union to the States, and working very successfully.

00:15:54.743 --> 00:16:02.703
And it turned out that the addition of these efforts
greatly enriches both programs from the technical side.

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:08.192
And after this experience of cooperation,
the people who took part in this

00:16:08.223 --> 00:16:11.663
became great enthusiasts of
further cooperation projects.

00:16:12.058 --> 00:16:17.585
And it seems to me that this cooperation
has a broader political meaning,

00:16:17.623 --> 00:16:28.184
because the more Russia and America cooperate,
the more people on both sides see the benefit

00:16:28.465 --> 00:16:35.839
and express their support for further cooperation,
which proves to be mutually beneficial.

00:16:35.839 --> 00:16:49.505
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:49.505 --> 00:16:54.155
Knowledge of the Russian language is absolutely
necessary for the history of cosmonautics, of course,

00:16:54.193 --> 00:16:57.933
because archival documents are in Russian.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:03.591
If you want to interview
someone, then, as a rule, veterans

00:17:03.591 --> 00:17:07.903
of the Soviet space program
do not know foreign languages,

00:17:08.829 --> 00:17:13.617
grew up in the Soviet Union where teaching
foreign languages was not well established

00:17:13.665 --> 00:17:16.776
and they didn't have much
opportunity to practice either.

00:17:19.492 --> 00:17:31.503
And it is very important to know the language well, for example,
in order to work with audio materials. In the space archive in Moscow

00:17:31.515 --> 00:17:38.132
I listened to recordings of the cosmonauts'
conversations with Earth, with the Mission Control Center.

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:46.941
This is, in general, an underexplored part
of the materials, and a very interesting one,

00:17:47.252 --> 00:17:55.510
because some issues of equipment
failures also pop up there

00:17:55.520 --> 00:18:02.879
as well as some kind of negotiations on the approval of certain
actions that the cosmonauts requested, and Earth gave them.

00:18:03.015 --> 00:18:10.314
This is where this social aspect of the relationship
between cosmonauts and engineers on Earth comes to light.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:18.271
And to understand all the subtleties of these negotiations,
a good knowledge of the Russian language is absolutely necessary.

00:18:19.377 --> 00:18:29.080
Well, in general, for interviewing, in order
for people to open up, to believe you,

00:18:29.882 --> 00:18:33.810
to tell you something that
they might not tell someone else,

00:18:34.132 --> 00:18:42.752
it is very important to establish a trusting relationship with them,
and a good command of the language is essential for this.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:48.292
This lets you win their trust
and allows you to get close enough

00:18:48.335 --> 00:18:56.298
so that this conversation becomes interesting and
touches on topics that go beyond a purely formal interview.

00:18:56.622 --> 00:19:10.665
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:19:10.665 --> 00:19:19.742
It seems to me that cooperation
between Russian and American,

00:19:19.792 --> 00:19:23.927
European, Chinese,
Indian space programs —

00:19:23.955 --> 00:19:27.020
is absolutely inevitable in the future.

00:19:27.405 --> 00:19:32.600
Even if now there is competition somewhere,
there will be cooperation later.

00:19:33.195 --> 00:19:38.116
Historical experience shows
that this always happens.

00:19:38.442 --> 00:19:49.475
And in this sense, knowledge of the Russian language will be critical,
and you will gain a great advantage thanks to this knowledge,

00:19:49.552 --> 00:19:56.384
because you will be the person with the expertise
needed to participate in this collaboration.

00:19:56.815 --> 00:20:03.861
And the Russian space program has accumulated
a lot of experience, very interesting technologies

00:20:03.905 --> 00:20:08.651
that will be interesting in this cooperation,

00:20:08.682 --> 00:20:15.828
and knowledge of the Russian language will
allow you to participate in such joint programs

00:20:16.957 --> 00:20:22.381
and, of course, it will be useful to anyone
who works in the space industry.

00:20:22.777 --> 00:20:36.577
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:36.577 --> 00:20:41.172
Over the past few years, new
things that have happened —

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:48.009
of course, it is the launch
of commercial manned spacecraft,

00:20:48.045 --> 00:20:56.736
launch into orbit and suborbital flights of three
commercial spacecraft have already taken place.

00:20:56.822 --> 00:21:03.753
This marks a certain transition of
cosmonautics from a purely state business,

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:08.942
financed by state governments,
to wide commercial use

00:21:08.967 --> 00:21:21.695
which, as a rule, is much more efficient, breeds more
innovations and develops much faster and more interestingly.

00:21:22.022 --> 00:21:26.384
Very often, the government first invests
in the development of some technology,

00:21:26.402 --> 00:21:31.222
and then business picks it up and
develops it and makes it for everyday use.

00:21:31.352 --> 00:21:39.467
Let's take computers: the first computers were financed
by the government for some kind of military calculations,

00:21:39.964 --> 00:21:46.002
and as soon as business began to use computers,
the range of their application immediately expanded,

00:21:46.027 --> 00:21:50.512
and now we have these computers and the like
not only on our desks but also in our hands.

00:21:52.752 --> 00:22:02.495
Space technology is also evolving from
purely propagandist and military applications

00:22:02.522 --> 00:22:13.240
to communications, to applications for some
kind of global signal exchange, for the Internet,

00:22:13.609 --> 00:22:16.192
and now for space tourism.

00:22:16.242 --> 00:22:20.909
And who knows what other applications
business will come up with that we all need?

00:22:21.079 --> 00:22:27.592
Therefore, it seems to me, from the viewpoint of
expanding the capabilities of manned cosmonautics

00:22:27.642 --> 00:22:31.979
new commercial spacecraft
play a key role here.

00:22:32.532 --> 00:22:38.839
But I also think it is very important
to develop automatic stations,

00:22:38.852 --> 00:22:44.881
and in particular the rovers that
are now exploring the surface of Mars

00:22:45.189 --> 00:22:51.232
because this year already, two new
rovers — a Chinese and an American one —

00:22:51.269 --> 00:22:58.882
landed on the surface of Mars, and they create
a completely different impression of space for us

00:22:58.932 --> 00:23:03.363
by broadcasting these pictures in good resolution,

00:23:04.844 --> 00:23:09.897
through which we now perceive the
space as something no longer alien,

00:23:09.922 --> 00:23:13.372
but as something that fits
into our perception of the world.

00:23:15.669 --> 00:23:19.913
And, of course, automatic devices
have much greater capabilities

00:23:23.301 --> 00:23:29.372
and allow for a wider range of scientific
observations than a person in space

00:23:29.409 --> 00:23:32.357
who is limited in their capacity.

00:23:32.394 --> 00:23:39.913
Most of the equipment of manned spacecraft
is spent on maintaining human life.

00:23:40.142 --> 00:23:43.702
Very few resources are
left for actual research.

00:23:44.082 --> 00:23:49.752
Therefore, from the viewpoint of scientific research
and expansion of our space knowledge in general,

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:57.144
the use of automatic spacecraft — it
seems the main path of development to me.

00:23:57.397 --> 00:24:10.614
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:24:10.614 --> 00:24:12.267
The project is called The Challenge.

00:24:12.682 --> 00:24:22.701
It seems to me that this is a very interesting turn in
the relationship between cosmonautics and popular culture.

00:24:23.427 --> 00:24:30.480
If earlier mass culture
followed cosmonautics,

00:24:31.102 --> 00:24:40.222
that is, some images and symbols of
cosmonautics circulated in popular culture,

00:24:40.247 --> 00:24:44.858
so, cosmonautics developed
for its own purposes,

00:24:45.284 --> 00:24:53.218
and mass culture perceived,
processed, distributed, reimagined it,

00:24:53.604 --> 00:24:59.406
then now, mass culture, in a sense,

00:24:59.701 --> 00:25:03.216
starts to use cosmonautics
to achieve its own goals,

00:25:04.531 --> 00:25:08.769
when they are able to
buy a flight to a space station,

00:25:08.806 --> 00:25:15.718
and it is a space station that is
being used as a movie set.

00:25:17.116 --> 00:25:26.051
And in this sense, cosmonautics is already becoming a somewhat
service industry for the mass media industry, mass culture.

00:25:26.829 --> 00:25:36.681
It seems to me that this changes the relationship
between these two sociocultural phenomena

00:25:38.556 --> 00:25:44.745
and generally shows the ever-increasing
role of mass media in our lives,

00:25:45.171 --> 00:25:49.956
when they subdue even such powerful
technological projects as space ones.

00:25:50.281 --> 00:26:03.616
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:03.616 --> 00:26:07.345
In the field of cosmonautics, I would
name 'Mars rover' the word of the year.

00:26:08.984 --> 00:26:20.362
It seems to me that it is these new automatic vehicles
roaming the expanses of Mars and bringing Mars to us,

00:26:20.871 --> 00:26:29.571
allowing us to be there, as it were —
this is a new concept that enters our lives.

00:26:29.621 --> 00:26:38.726
If before, there was a lunar rover, which was
a propaganda project of the Soviet government,

00:26:38.776 --> 00:26:43.546
diverting attention from
the loss of the lunar race,

00:26:43.971 --> 00:26:47.594
then now, the Mars rover is actually
a triumph for all mankind,

00:26:47.646 --> 00:26:53.840
when both American and Chinese machines
are now roaming the expanses of Mars.

00:26:54.756 --> 00:27:00.344
For us, 'Mars rover' is a word that, it seems to me,
is already becoming a household name in a way.

00:27:00.344 --> 00:27:08.906
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:27:08.906 --> 00:27:09.581
Books.

00:27:09.581 --> 00:27:17.876
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:27:17.876 --> 00:27:25.768
This is a difficult question. Probably two books:
The Master and Margarita by Bulgakov and The Gift by Nabokov.

00:27:26.316 --> 00:27:34.904
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:27:34.904 --> 00:27:42.272
Probably, it is the physicist Landau who was not
only a brilliant physicist, but also a great wit.

00:27:42.634 --> 00:27:51.421
It was he who said that the sciences are
divided into natural, unnatural (humanities)

00:27:51.559 --> 00:27:54.559
and against nature
(such as Marxism and Leninism).

00:27:54.559 --> 00:28:03.314
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:28:03.314 --> 00:28:08.862
Try to come up with something new.
Learn. And don't be afraid to make mistakes.

00:28:08.862 --> 00:28:16.914
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:28:16.914 --> 00:28:17.701
To Mars.

00:28:17.701 --> 00:28:27.901
